WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.749
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:10.598
I want to first start off by saying
that you have easily been one of

00:00:10.598 --> 00:00:15.420
the most instrumental leaders
in shaping my journey.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.440
And you've even been responsible for my
journey here to Stanford Business School,

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.630
so, it's truly an honor to be here today.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.670
And I think I speak for
many of the people here in this audience,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.781
that it's truly an honor.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:29.781 --> 00:00:31.795
&gt;&gt; So I'd like to start with one of

00:00:31.795 --> 00:00:37.660
the most compelling pieces of advice
I've heard from any business leader.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.600
You've mentioned that the three most
important skills in business are to read,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.520
count and sell.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.440
Take us back to the early
days of your career, and

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.800
how you cultivated these skills and used
them to become a successful entrepreneur.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.200
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all, it's indeed a great

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:01.010
privilege and honor to be here,
thank you for inviting me.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.490
In particular,
my colleague and dear friend,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:08.760
a chairman of Stanford, and
Jeff Raikes, thank you, Jeff.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.785
Jeff and I work on the Alliance for
Green Revolution in Africa,

00:01:13.785 --> 00:01:19.480
snd he invited us here,
told our board meeting.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.430
So, I've got a number of my
colleagues from the board.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.460
And Judith Rodin I see there, Jozette and

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.410
Lina, thank you so
much for joining us today.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.730
Well, you got to be able to read,
you got to be able to count and

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:42.559
you got to be able to sell, in reality
that's what we as entrepreneurs do.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:47.850
When I was a little boy, my mother,

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.950
she came up with this idea that I
should earn my own pocket money.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:00.230
So, she brought me a box
of chewing gum and

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:04.280
started selling them to
my school In my school.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:11.840
But I got lost on the way back home
because I couldn't read that well,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.470
because I was only little.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.370
So I quickly learned that
you got to be able to sell.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:22.882
I could count, but I couldn't read before,
that's probably why I did math and

00:02:22.882 --> 00:02:24.993
engineering.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So,

00:02:24.993 --> 00:02:31.210
let's talk a little bit more
about entrepreneurship.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.260
It's easy for

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.720
a lot of us to think of entrepreneurship
from a more current context.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.510
It's such a hot topic and

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:44.490
respected career path here with thousands
of tech startups emerging each year.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:49.290
But let's go back to Zimbabwe in the early
90s when you first had the vision for

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.430
Eco net.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:55.560
What gave you the courage to take on
such a daring and uncommon career path?

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.830
&gt;&gt; Well, by the time I decided to set up

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:07.620
Econet in 1993, I had been in business for
about six or seven years.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.800
So I had a well-established business,
because I started in 1986, so

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.290
I was actually 25.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.090
And so

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:24.000
I had built up a fairly
successful construction business,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.080
I'm an engineer in telecommunications.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.590
So the whole mobile revolution

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.350
was something I'd been tracking
since school actually.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:40.630
When I was at university we
already knew what was going on,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.390
like most engineers do, it's school.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.190
If you want to know what's going
on in artificial intelligence,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.930
talk to the guys at Stanford, they'll tell
you what's going to happen in ten years.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:58.030
So we knew this was going to happen and
it's going to be big.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.310
But 1993 was particularly
important in my tracking.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:06.990
Because that's when they went to using the
digital standard, which were called GSM,

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.810
and I immediately knew this
was the time to get in.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:17.230
And I thought it was going
to be a fairly simple thing,

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:22.200
you know you,
I went to my former employers.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.100
And I said hey guys this thing
is going to happen now I'd

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.610
like to do a joined venture with you.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:34.520
And I used to have a letter I think
someone from the intelligence stole it.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.270
But I used to have a letter which used
to be on my wall written by the head of

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:39.430
the telephone company.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:45.320
Back to me to say this is a fad,
it's going to disappear,

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.570
we have more important things to do here,
so don't bother us.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:54.760
So I went back and I said okay since you
don't want to be bothered, let me do it.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.540
They said well no you can't do that,

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.640
because then you would
violate our monopoly.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.256
I says so how can you monopoly
something you don't do?

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.570
So I did the smartest thing
which is hire a lawyer.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:15.990
Right, I went to a lawyer I said
do they really have a monopoly?

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.970
He says no they don't but

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:27.380
if you really want to take them on,
there's easier thing to do.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:33.450
Just steal a tank and go park it in
front of the President's office,

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:38.070
that's a much quicker,
because that's how he's going to react.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.485
And somebody did it to him
about three weeks ago.

00:05:40.485 --> 00:05:46.240
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.690
&gt;&gt; So, we won't go into that.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:58.570
So, anyway I took him to court and

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:03.120
I hired a lawyer from New York,

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.780
an amazing American lawyer
called Judith O'Neil.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:11.750
And she was a specialist in
telecommunications law, but

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.720
she couldn't practice in our jurisdiction.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.129
And she read the law and
she said to me, you know,

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.830
you've got a point,
I'll help you with this.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:31.280
So she actually used to come to and
advise my legal team

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.890
on this battle, and it took us five years.
&gt;&gt; So

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:43.272
at the time you were trying to
bring in a service when 75%

00:06:43.272 --> 00:06:48.520
of Africans had not heard
a telephone ringing.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:54.015
So you were trying to solve a problem
that Africans did not yet know they had.

00:06:54.015 --> 00:06:57.265
How did you gain the boldness and
the conviction

00:06:57.265 --> 00:07:02.065
to take on such a problem at the time?
&gt;&gt; You know when I worked as

00:07:02.065 --> 00:07:06.925
a telephone engineer, you can imagine,

00:07:06.925 --> 00:07:10.815
we used to take out adverts,
the government used to.

00:07:10.815 --> 00:07:13.499
Saying to people you talk
too much on the phone.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:20.453
The ads used to say don't jabber jabber so
much to the customers.

00:07:20.453 --> 00:07:26.835
[LAUGH] So
I mean to get a when you work for

00:07:26.835 --> 00:07:34.828
the telephone company
in any African country.

00:07:34.828 --> 00:07:38.680
One of the great,
whenever you walk down the street,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:43.960
people would come up to you and say,
can you help me get a telephone line?

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.985
It was a badge of honor.

00:07:45.985 --> 00:07:52.780
And a lot of my friends made money out
of it, it was called corruption, but

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:57.900
it was, it could take, officially,
14 years to get a telephone.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:04.100
In most African countries our tele-density
was point zero seven percent.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:10.400
So it was literally impossible for
anyone to have a telephone line.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:15.820
And when we went to
the constitutional court

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:21.110
we took this thing through the lower
courts and of course, be honest,

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:25.930
by the time we got to constitutional
court I was public enemy number one.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:29.070
I mean it wasn't funny
I used to get arrested.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.570
So but

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:38.410
when we finally get to the constitutional
court we had to get evidence.

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:40.800
For our courts to even consider.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.860
It's not very different from
your own Supreme Court and

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.530
we were to face a nine member bench and

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.820
the matter was no longer
about the monopoly.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.900
We had taken it was a matter
of freedom of expression.

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:04.800
And so we said look.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.160
75% of the people in this country
have never heard a telephone ringing.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.860
That was the headline of.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:15.525
And that captured headlines across
the entire African continent.

00:09:15.525 --> 00:09:20.270
75% of our people have never
heard a telephone ringing.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:26.580
And people were calling me and
said where did you get this stastic from?

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.030
And I showed them where we got it.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.510
We had to gather this research.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.630
Which is very important as
an entrepreneur, you got to get your data.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:42.030
So, the quote pulled down the monopoly and

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:48.670
for me it was really,
we were just trying to respond to a need.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.940
The revolution for
me in telecommunications in Africa.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:01.715
When we look back now,
that is just over 20 years ago.

00:10:01.715 --> 00:10:07.470
75% of the African people
have a telephone now.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.130
Not just ring but they own one.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:17.440
Cause our teledensity now is 75 percent,
continentally.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.880
Some countries like Botswana, well,
you know, they're 120%, 130%.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:27.320
I don't know how they do that stat but.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.170
&gt;&gt; In Nigeria,

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.650
I know how they do it because
it's the robes you see, so

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:35.294
they have one phone in here and
the other one in this one.

00:10:35.294 --> 00:10:41.760
[LAUGH] It's a remarkable revolution for

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.860
our industry.
&gt;&gt; It seems like every step of the way,

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:50.210
building trustworthy partnerships
were fundamental to your success.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:55.220
Whether it was gaining the trust of
agents who enable mobile money to work or

00:10:55.220 --> 00:11:00.310
the trust of the hundreds of young people
who first spread the message of Econet

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:02.130
during the early days.

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:08.220
How did you get people to trust you?
&gt;&gt; You know,

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:15.390
I think the key is by not being
transactional in relationships.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:20.210
And it goes broader than just
your relationship with customers.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:28.170
Getting people to accept mobile
money rather than mobile phones

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:31.330
was the most counterintuitive
thing we ever had to do

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:37.900
because you're talking to people who
don't have money or very little money.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:44.070
And they also had no bank accounts.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:51.100
So we had to persaude them that
they could give us their money.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:57.870
In the case of Zimbabwe,
they had faced 500,000% hyperinflation.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.460
They knew if you put money in the bank.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:01.790
The next day you didn't have it.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:08.660
And we had to launch mobile
money during that time.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.412
So I used to say to my people,

00:12:10.412 --> 00:12:15.810
I'd say, when we're discussing how
do you take mobile money across.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.900
How do we get people to accept it?

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:23.990
It's just like trying to get people to
drink how did they do that Coca-Cola,

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.510
a black drink in a bottle?

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.180
But they did it, right?

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:33.450
They build this incredible company,
Coca-Cola.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.210
I mean,
that must be the most difficult thing,

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:38.480
to get somebody that
black stuff you drink.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.780
Well, we got people to open bank accounts,
millions of them.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.270
And one ways we used to do it was,

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:53.380
you're familiar, many of you,
with what are called matatus in Africa.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:54.770
The little combis.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:13:00.780
So we went to the universities and
we hired the university students.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:06.240
And we wanted them to ride on matatus.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:10.260
So they'd ride two by two in matatus.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:15.650
And do you know how noisy and
lively African buses are?

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:17.220
We talk to each other.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.530
And so get onto the bus.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:25.920
And you start, they're supposed to strike
a conversation about mobile money.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:30.955
I'm sending money to my cousin on the
other side of town, the whole bus stops.

00:13:30.955 --> 00:13:34.800
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.090
&gt;&gt; And they talk,

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:37.470
they get off the next bus stop,

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:40.860
they get onto the next one,
and they had to do this.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:45.850
And we had a calculation that said,
we needed them to

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:50.500
talk to one and a half million
people to get to our tipping point.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.770
There's no marketing, that was it.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:58.570
Those guys on the buses, talking and
getting off the next one.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:01.835
And people didn't realize
they were the same guys.

00:14:01.835 --> 00:14:08.751
[LAUGH] Coming on the bus and
having an argument about.

00:14:08.751 --> 00:14:12.780
And we also set a street comedy.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:16.400
Where someone will say
you've stolen my money and

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:20.115
they start a mock fight in
the middle of a township.

00:14:21.305 --> 00:14:23.950
And people will always gather
when there's a fight, right?

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:26.915
[LAUGH] So,

00:14:26.915 --> 00:14:31.135
they then explain that actually you can't
steal my money because it's in my phone.

00:14:32.805 --> 00:14:35.775
And that got people to go out and
register.

00:14:36.965 --> 00:14:40.955
So these are just techniques
that our people work on.

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:42.820
&gt;&gt; Amazing.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.170
Here at Stanford Business School,

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:51.230
we celebrate failure, we even go as
far as to say it is necessary for

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:56.130
growth and success and
to look at a leader such as yourself,

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:01.040
it can be extremely tempting to
view you as absent of failure.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.650
Given that entrepreneurship
one of the hardest parts is

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.750
managing your own psychology.

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:09.800
Can you talk to us about how
you've navigated failure and

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:13.240
self doubt.
&gt;&gt; Well I wouldn't say I've ever

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:22.110
celebrated failure, but
I've run into it now and again.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.450
Let's say that, okay.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:29.100
And probably the most spectacular
was my entry into Nigeria.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.600
Now that was pretty spectacular.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.740
We went in just after the end
of military rule in Nigeria,

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.946
this would be I guess 99, 2000.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:49.720
Because one in five black people
in the world is a Nigerian, and

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:55.750
this is some market, and we were trying
to get in, and we went and of course

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:03.530
We had to build a consortium, and
Nigeria was particularly difficult.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:08.110
Today you've got billions of
dollars going into Nigeria.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:15.430
But if you look back in 2000, 2001,
there's no noble industry in Nigeria.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:20.950
And no one was putting money into Nigeria.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:26.047
I remember going around the banks
in South Africa, and investors and

00:16:26.047 --> 00:16:27.660
they said are you mad?

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:29.057
You want to go to Nigeria?

00:16:29.057 --> 00:16:31.258
I said we have to be in Nigeria.

00:16:31.258 --> 00:16:37.298
If you're going to be in business in
Africa, you have to be in Nigeria period.

00:16:37.298 --> 00:16:44.514
So, we went in and we build a consortium
of Nigerian high net worth businesses,

00:16:44.514 --> 00:16:49.700
and business leaders, and so
forth to bid for a license.

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:52.790
And the government had
set up an auction system.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:58.527
And they became the most expensive,
even to this day,

00:16:58.527 --> 00:17:03.369
licenses ever issued In sub-Sahara,
south of the Sahara.

00:17:03.369 --> 00:17:06.440
I know in Tunisia it's
something paid more for it.

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:11.770
But we paid in 2001 $285 million for
a license.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:19.856
And a year into the business,
one of my partners walked in and

00:17:19.856 --> 00:17:25.419
put a bill on the table for
one of the state governors.

00:17:25.419 --> 00:17:31.560
And said you gotta pay us each $4 million
or we throw you out of the country.

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:35.630
So, I got thrown out of the country.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:43.084
And pretty much got kicked
out of that investment.

00:17:43.084 --> 00:17:48.549
It took me ten years of
international arbitration

00:17:48.549 --> 00:17:54.160
to win back our investment
that we had lost.

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:59.800
And along the way I had
that governor jJailed.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:03.468
Spent thirteen years in
British prison after making

00:18:03.468 --> 00:18:07.730
the mistake of going to the Biag,
got picked up by Interpol.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:17.650
I mean, it's a known fact that I was
a witness against them for corruption.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:22.420
So that was bittersweet,
very difficult time for us.

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:28.510
But there were a lot of
entrepreneurial lessons.

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:33.560
And after fives years,
we went back into Nigeria.

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:37.810
And the fastest growing parts of our
businesses are in Nigeria today.

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:43.114
So we learned a lot that was really,
really difficult.

00:18:43.114 --> 00:18:46.331
And there were no celebrations.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:18:46.331 --> 00:18:48.293
&gt;&gt; Except when I got the Arbitral Award,

00:18:48.293 --> 00:18:50.450
which is the biggest in African history.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:57.230
So there was a nice end to it.
&gt;&gt; You've been saught

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:03.780
out by many leaders such as Barrack Obama,
but who do you look to for inspiration?

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:07.770
Are there any open coming young leaders
on the continent that inspire you

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:12.473
about the future of Africa?
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:19:12.473 --> 00:19:16.379
When I started that Facebook page,
my daughter,

00:19:16.379 --> 00:19:20.750
I didn't know that my daughter
was at college in the US,

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:25.740
was sharing things I was sending her,
with her friends.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:27.110
So she started saying to me, but

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:31.180
you know, my friends think
you should talk about this.

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:32.751
I said, what friends?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:19:32.751 --> 00:19:35.625
&gt;&gt; She said, well, we share everything.

00:19:35.625 --> 00:19:41.050
So we then got to start
working on this Facebook page.

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:45.960
And the most interesting part for
me is reading the comments.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:54.030
I just get absolutely inspired by
the comments of young Africans

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:59.890
from virtually every corner of
the continent who are running businesses.

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:04.990
In extraordinary situations,
that even I couldn't have imagined.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:07.950
And they're making a success of it.

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:14.370
So I really get inspired by what
young entrepreneurs are doing.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:18.690
And I'm in constant conversation
with them on Facebook.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:21.204
I even know some of them.

00:20:21.204 --> 00:20:23.860
I go into meetings, I say,
hey, where is Austin?

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:27.024
Stuff like this and they come.

00:20:27.024 --> 00:20:28.716
Because they are doing amazing things.

00:20:28.716 --> 00:20:33.694
And basically you've got
people like Juliana Rotich.

00:20:33.694 --> 00:20:38.184
The young Kenyan woman who built Ushahidi,

00:20:38.184 --> 00:20:42.550
we got Fred,is Fred here today?

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:47.170
Fred Swanika, the African
Leadership Academy many of you know

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:51.490
Fred is an alumni of Stanford.

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:54.800
I mean these are the guys who are going to

00:20:56.150 --> 00:21:01.010
totally transform our continent and
the world to be honest.

00:21:02.625 --> 00:21:09.020
So it's not so much,
I mean it's a great privilege of course,

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:13.560
when you can have a conversation
with people like President Obama,

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.140
who I knew before he was President.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:19.455
And I was reminded that
I'm eight months older.

00:21:19.455 --> 00:21:21.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:22.645
&gt;&gt; Just, you know,

00:21:22.645 --> 00:21:27.921
this helps you to get a stability and
manage such a great success.

00:21:27.921 --> 00:21:32.454
But in Africa it matters,
I'm senior brother.

00:21:32.454 --> 00:21:38.247
[LAUGH] So no, but really it's

00:21:38.247 --> 00:21:45.290
people I work with over the years.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.390
People like,
Doctor Judith Rodin, she's here.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:52.490
She's the former president of
the Rockefeller Foundation.

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:57.320
She's been an amazing mentor to me,
over the last ten years,

00:21:57.320 --> 00:21:59.330
particularly in
understanding philanthropy.

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:05.980
Because many of us as entrepreneurs,
we set out to go into philanthropy.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:09.006
We actually don't know how it works.

00:22:09.006 --> 00:22:15.090
And you need to be talking to people
like Doctor Rodin to understand.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:21.150
So it's a lot of people that I work with
over a very, very long time that I turn

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:26.650
to when I face challenges.
&gt;&gt; Shifting to philanthropy a bit.

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:30.525
In addition to your success
as an entrepreneur,

00:22:30.525 --> 00:22:37.716
you've funded the education of over 40,000
children across Zimbabwe and Africa.

00:22:37.716 --> 00:22:41.780
How must it feel to have come so
far from humble beginnings and

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:47.060
what has motivated you to give
back in such an extraordinary way?

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:48.527
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all,

00:22:48.527 --> 00:22:53.500
I'm really excited this week because
we just got an announcement that we

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:56.608
got a Rhode Scholar out
of program this year.

00:22:56.608 --> 00:23:01.640
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:03.000
&gt;&gt; And we got one last year.

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:09.280
And I think that's our third or
fourth now.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:14.260
So, and it began,
like a lot of things I don't

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:20.160
think my wife and I sat down and
said, let's give back.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.580
Because, to be honest with you,
we started when we didn't have any money.

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:28.526
So there's almost nothing to give
back if you think of it that way.

00:23:28.526 --> 00:23:32.298
There was a crisis In Africa at that time.

00:23:32.298 --> 00:23:36.420
Which was HIV/AIDS.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:41.920
People were dying on
a scale you cannot imagine.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:46.890
If in my small business I was burying

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:52.035
members of my workforce every week.

00:23:52.035 --> 00:23:54.610
We actually thought we
were all going to die.

00:23:54.610 --> 00:24:00.300
That's how it felt living
in Africa in 1990,

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.962
91 because of the HIV crisis.

00:24:03.962 --> 00:24:08.330
And what made the crisis so
difficult were the children.

00:24:09.350 --> 00:24:11.180
What would we do with the children?

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:15.840
I knew what happens when
children don't go to school.

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:20.390
We had Liberia to tell us about that.

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:24.310
Child soliders, all sorts of challenges.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:28.890
You could rip apart a country if
these kids did not go to school.

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:36.460
So for us, it was responding to that and
we came up with this model.

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:38.990
Which my wife then said she
was going to run full time.

00:24:40.276 --> 00:24:46.110
It was basically,
if we can find a relative to the child

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:51.830
rather than try to institutionalize any
children, let's find their relatives and

00:24:51.830 --> 00:24:55.660
tell their relatives we will
meet the economic side.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:57.320
We'll pay for school fees.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:59.583
We'll give you some food.

00:24:59.583 --> 00:25:02.690
We'll help with clothing, and so forth.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:06.925
So it began as a little program,
really around our own employees.

00:25:06.925 --> 00:25:11.821
And we probably had 50
when my wife kind of

00:25:11.821 --> 00:25:15.765
began to do it on a full time basis.

00:25:15.765 --> 00:25:17.984
And she'd come from work and

00:25:17.984 --> 00:25:24.070
she would just really just make
sure these kids were looked after.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:30.720
And one day I just walked in and said
actually they're 5,000 that we now help.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:34.084
And we've extended beyond the company
because people keep bringing

00:25:34.084 --> 00:25:34.960
these kids to us.

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:39.410
So we built it from there.

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:43.703
And as of this year,
twenty-odd years into it,

00:25:43.703 --> 00:25:51.100
we've assisted 250,000 that went
through our system of education.

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:56.980
We began to wherever we went the first
thing we told our management teams,

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:59.682
if we went to Botswana or
Zambia or any country,

00:25:59.682 --> 00:26:03.960
you've gotta set up the program,
you've gotta support it.

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:08.195
So we support it across every
single country where we operate.

00:26:08.195 --> 00:26:14.210
And then, we select from those kids,
the really high performers.

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:18.430
What do you do if it's 16 and
you got a genius on your hands?

00:26:18.430 --> 00:26:19.390
Do you say go home?

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:22.850
We just treat them like our own kids.

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:28.310
We help them apply for scholarships
to Stanford and Harvard and wherever.

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:33.978
So we have 354 in the United States
this year, of our kids.

00:26:33.978 --> 00:26:39.936
And we'be got some in China,
some in the UK and so forth and

00:26:39.936 --> 00:26:46.145
we helped them along and so
it's been a great program to see.

00:26:46.145 --> 00:26:54.840
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:58.200
&gt;&gt; You've mentioned that entrepreneurship

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:03.622
is not just about making money, but
about being an agent for change.

00:27:03.622 --> 00:27:08.440
There's many people here in this audience
who have larger dreams of going into

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:12.090
the world and being catalysts for
transformative change.

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:17.100
What do you think we should know,
and most importantly today,

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:22.360
what is your call to action for us?
&gt;&gt; First of all,

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:28.090
don't wait until you've made your billion,
okay?

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:31.523
I know, everyone at Stanford ends
up making a billion once they're

00:27:31.523 --> 00:27:33.432
entrepreneurs, right?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:27:33.432 --> 00:27:34.440
&gt;&gt; I mean, that's why you come

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:35.442
to Stanford, that's right?

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:39.790
So look,
you're almost guaranteed to be successful.

00:27:41.180 --> 00:27:42.410
So start straight away.

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:44.920
Why do you have to wait?

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:48.480
As you register your company,
register your foundation.

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:50.853
Register your philanthropy.

00:27:50.853 --> 00:27:52.420
And it's actually good for you.

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:58.189
In our house,
we actually never discuss business.

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:05.000
We discuss the things we're
doing around philanthropy.

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:09.540
With my kids,
in fact it's very difficult with my

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:14.105
kids because they're more interested
in the philanthropy side.

00:28:14.105 --> 00:28:19.675
Then, maybe they, during holidays,
they prefer to go work for their mother.

00:28:19.675 --> 00:28:24.545
Very few volunteer to go and
sell in one of my shops.

00:28:24.545 --> 00:28:30.214
[LAUGH] But
the reality is these things in today's

00:28:30.214 --> 00:28:35.530
society, we have to start straight away.

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.780
There's no reason for you to wait.

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:43.720
You've got to find something that,
you've gotta find a cause.

00:28:43.720 --> 00:28:46.665
If you don't find a cause,

00:28:46.665 --> 00:28:52.052
you'll find that making money is a self.

00:28:52.052 --> 00:28:54.200
You've gotta get into your cause now.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:55.520
What is it you're interested in?

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:58.170
Is it the environment?

00:28:58.170 --> 00:28:59.610
Is it the climate change?

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:05.240
Is it, there is something that should
capture your interest every day.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:11.530
And for me,
I can't wait at the end of my day

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:16.560
to escape into discussing
the issues of philanthropy.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:22.260
That's why I'm here, what we're here to
discuss, small holder farmers in Africa.

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:26.531
So make it an integral part
of what you are doing.

00:29:26.531 --> 00:29:30.712
If there's an even more
excellent way as they say,

00:29:30.712 --> 00:29:33.540
why not just do the philanthropy?

00:29:35.650 --> 00:29:42.759
Because if you feel you can make a change
without going off to running for

00:29:42.759 --> 00:29:48.503
profit to do it, that's my counsel.
&gt;&gt; Final question before we open it up so

00:29:48.503 --> 00:29:51.195
some of our business students here for
questions,

00:29:51.195 --> 00:29:55.326
what do you think are some of the most
pressing global problems that we should be

00:29:55.326 --> 00:30:01.164
thinking about today?
&gt;&gt; Wow, [LAUGH].

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:06.800
We always think don't we
that we'd like to hand our

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:11.650
children fewer problems
than were handed to us.

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:18.940
Our parents kind of handed
over a world where they say,

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:21.860
well, we've achieved
the Soviet Union is gone.

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:25.566
because we lived under the Cold War and
not so

00:30:25.566 --> 00:30:30.930
sure we are handing over a great world to
you at the moment, I'm sorry about that.

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:35.693
You've got a few problems that
you're going to have to deal with.

00:30:35.693 --> 00:30:40.723
Of course, top of mind for
me is climate change,

00:30:40.723 --> 00:30:43.800
because it doesn't give us.

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:48.240
We don't have a planet B somebody said.

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:53.650
We don't get another shot at it,
if we don't get into it now,

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:57.380
so it's up there.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:05.040
From the African perspective, it's jobs.

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:10.490
One would argue it's global,
but for your generation

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:19.050
it's bigger than decolonization
ever was as a challenge.

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:25.677
We have a continent where
the average age is 19.

00:31:25.677 --> 00:31:30.836
We have 1.2 billion,
by some estimates we'll be

00:31:30.836 --> 00:31:37.750
4 billion by the turn of the century,
in the lives of most of you.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:43.380
If we don't create jobs, we end up with.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:44.340
It's as simple as that.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:48.120
And they can sweep all
the way down to the South.

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:55.720
So the security nexus
is linked to the jobs.

00:31:57.170 --> 00:31:59.090
We have to create hope.

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:03.734
When I think of the people trying
to cross the Mediterranean,

00:32:03.734 --> 00:32:09.538
sometimes you read reports of 500
drownings a week in the Mediterranean,

00:32:09.538 --> 00:32:13.845
we almost forget that they had
to cross the Sahara first.

00:32:17.347 --> 00:32:20.772
To get to the Mediterranean.

00:32:20.772 --> 00:32:28.960
And yet, we are the continent with so
much resources, so much capacity.

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:36.290
So it's an extraordinary charge for us,
while we are still able to do something,

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:41.720
but an even greater challenge for
our children, but it's also global.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:46.640
I think the stability of the world,
the future of the world,

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:50.819
will to great extent be shaped
by our response to this.

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:56.610
So we have to pick up every
too,l everything that we can,

00:32:56.610 --> 00:33:00.290
to try and
take on some of these challenges.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:02.081
The rest, well they're Stanford,

00:33:02.081 --> 00:33:07.190
you know them.
&gt;&gt; Amazing.

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:08.905
So now we'll take some
questions from the audience.

00:33:14.915 --> 00:33:15.606
Hi Strive.

00:33:15.606 --> 00:33:18.733
Thank you so much for being here,
I really appreciate it.

00:33:18.733 --> 00:33:22.841
I was just wondering,
on either a personal or

00:33:22.841 --> 00:33:27.391
professional level,
what keeps you up at night?

00:33:27.391 --> 00:33:29.849
What do you wake up in the morning
really concerned about,

00:33:29.849 --> 00:33:34.358
thinking about?
&gt;&gt; You know,

00:33:34.358 --> 00:33:38.772
I never keep up at night.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:38.772 --> 00:33:41.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] President Obama said to

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:43.230
me once, what keeps you up at night?

00:33:43.230 --> 00:33:43.980
I said nothing.

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:46.055
I'm not running the world, you are.

00:33:46.055 --> 00:33:48.746
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:48.746 --> 00:33:49.470
&gt;&gt; He said, okay,

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:52.838
suppose you woke up in
the middle of the night.

00:33:52.838 --> 00:33:57.050
So okay, jobs,
how do we create employment?

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:07.230
That really is a challenge to me,
how do we get our young people.

00:34:07.230 --> 00:34:14.890
So now I'm persuaded that the answer lies
in part with entrepreneurship, okay?

00:34:14.890 --> 00:34:17.160
If I can't create somebody a job,

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:22.490
maybe I can help them create the jobs,
inspire them to go out and do something.

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:28.086
And I have an exciting job to do,
which does

00:34:28.086 --> 00:34:32.124
give me a lot of energy in the morning.

00:34:38.151 --> 00:34:41.860
&gt;&gt; We have a question right up here.
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:44.480
Thank you so much for this opportunity.

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:48.355
I had always dream to
come very close to you.

00:34:48.355 --> 00:34:50.180
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:50.180 --> 00:34:52.490
&gt;&gt; My name is Brian and I'm from Zimbabwe.

00:34:52.490 --> 00:34:58.170
So my question was, I understand after you
finished your undergraduate in Germany,

00:34:58.170 --> 00:35:03.290
or somewhere in Europe, I'm no longer
sure, you went back to Zimbabwe to

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:07.690
work in Africa, and then start to
build your ideas from the continent.

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:11.650
What advice would you give to someone
who is graduating right now, or

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:18.577
maybe to be specific, graduating in 2020?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:18.577 --> 00:35:20.293
&gt;&gt; Do you think it's best for us,

00:35:20.293 --> 00:35:24.787
if we dream of changing our continent
through entrepreneurship, to first start

00:35:24.787 --> 00:35:29.212
working and gain some skills, or to go and
work maybe in past titles that we think

00:35:29.212 --> 00:35:33.830
would want to change in the future.
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:35.940
thank you, that's quite multifaceted.

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:42.580
I think there's no simple answer to this.

00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:48.350
I wish I could say that, but
what I can say to you, if you have

00:35:48.350 --> 00:35:53.990
an intention to get home and work there,
the sooner you go there, the better.

00:35:55.370 --> 00:36:00.280
It's actually very difficult
if you spend five,

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:03.850
ten years, you've got a family,
you've got roots, and so forth.

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:05.810
That's harder to do.

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:09.920
I just picked up my bag,
and I went home, okay?

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:13.170
And it was easy.

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:18.429
I think it would have been much harder
if it had been ten years later,

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:26.340
but having said that, I know a lot
of friends who came after they

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:32.620
had spent some time building up experience
and so forth, and did very well.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:37.159
But I also go so far as to say that I'll
never condemn somebody who doesn't come.

00:36:39.316 --> 00:36:44.436
Okay, the Africans living and
working in the diaspora

00:36:44.436 --> 00:36:49.221
are contributing as much
to Africa today as anybody

00:36:49.221 --> 00:36:54.022
who's there, and
I'm very proud of what they do.

00:36:56.948 --> 00:37:01.292
We can't accommodate a lot of these guys,
even if we wanted to, but

00:37:01.292 --> 00:37:05.500
they are sending money home,
they are keeping people in school.

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:12.170
The burden they carry for such a small
group of people is extraordinary.

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:19.870
So that's why I said there's no simple
answer, I wish I could say there was.

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:27.160
All the above will work hard, but
I wouldn't send you to a country at war.

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:28.230
That might be an issue.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:33.830
I had to leave Zimbabwe 18 years ago,
haven't been back, even for one day.

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:39.943
Okay, so there are always issues
that one needs to take into

00:37:39.943 --> 00:37:46.354
consideration.
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:37:46.354 --> 00:37:47.165
Hi Strive.

00:37:47.165 --> 00:37:50.267
Hi.
&gt;&gt; My name is Tunde, I'm from Nigeria.

00:37:50.267 --> 00:37:51.440
&gt;&gt; I figured, Tunde.

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:52.375
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:37:52.375 --> 00:37:54.589
&gt;&gt; There are just just some names,

00:37:54.589 --> 00:37:58.453
you know, Tunde from Nigeria,
Tendai from Zimbabwe.

00:37:58.453 --> 00:37:59.486
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:37:59.486 --> 00:38:03.110
&gt;&gt; You never get a Tendai from Kenya.

00:38:03.110 --> 00:38:04.711
&gt;&gt; I think I know a Tende from Nigeria

00:38:04.711 --> 00:38:05.620
though.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:07.990
&gt;&gt; Anyways, thank you for

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:08.888
this opportunity.

00:38:08.888 --> 00:38:14.060
Kind of piggy-backing on your point,
have you ever thought

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:19.840
about initiatives like what Africans and
the diaspora can

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:25.350
do to support developmental
goals in Africa?

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:30.250
Most of entrepreneurship, rather than what
you mention in terms of philanthropy or

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:34.685
supporting folks back home,
like investments and stuff like that.

00:38:34.685 --> 00:38:39.490
because from what you said,
not only do we have people here,

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:43.530
we have tremendous talent that work for
tough companies and

00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:47.830
stuff like that.
&gt;&gt; And

00:38:47.830 --> 00:38:52.700
there is a lot of conversation
around this issue today, okay.

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:59.082
How can we encourage,
we call it the diaspora,

00:38:59.082 --> 00:39:04.000
which means we include people
like Lebron James, by the way.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:05.852
He's an African in the diaspora.

00:39:05.852 --> 00:39:08.810
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:08.810 --> 00:39:10.290
&gt;&gt; So in Africa,

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:14.390
we think of all black people around
the world as part of the African diaspora,

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:18.080
but Africa is not just black people.

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:22.620
We have a wonderful
diversity on our continent.

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:27.400
But on the serious note,
there's a lot of conversation around

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:35.050
encouraging those who are living and
working outside Africa

00:39:35.050 --> 00:39:40.560
to invest in creating businesses and
supporting entrepreneurship.

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:46.000
Even investing through
local stock exchanges and

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:49.580
so forth, trying to mobilize capital.

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:51.180
I'm a great supporter of that, but

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:56.405
I haven't seen anything that I could say
to you, this is the perfect solution.

00:39:56.405 --> 00:40:03.580
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; Hi [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:03.580 --> 00:40:04.770
My name is Ruth.

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:07.000
I'm from Guana.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.730
I was wondering, as a chairman of

00:40:12.440 --> 00:40:15.720
what you think is the future
of small farming in Africa?

00:40:15.720 --> 00:40:20.360
And then thinking about that,
how do you think we are going to meet for

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:24.730
security challenges?
&gt;&gt; You should listen to the radio

00:40:24.730 --> 00:40:28.465
interview that Jeff and
I did this morning at 7:00 o'clock.

00:40:28.465 --> 00:40:29.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:31.815
&gt;&gt; Jeff Rakes, please Jeff, stand up.

00:40:31.815 --> 00:40:37.720
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:40.490
&gt;&gt; He's the Chairman of your

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:42.120
esteemed university.

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:47.380
But I've been a member of the board for
many, many years and help set it up.

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:51.720
And we spent goodness, an hour and

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:55.620
a half and you really gotta
listen to what Jeff had to say.

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.016
because I was just the backup.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:58.016 --> 00:41:00.947
&gt;&gt; I'm a city boy, I'm not a farmer.

00:41:00.947 --> 00:41:03.478
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:41:03.478 --> 00:41:06.408
&gt;&gt; But I've learned a few things and

00:41:06.408 --> 00:41:13.629
amongst them of course, 70% of our food
production in Africa is done by women,

00:41:13.629 --> 00:41:17.620
small holder farmers, and they are ageing.

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:21.790
It's a paradox for us.

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:27.500
We've got millions of young
people without jobs or

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:30.740
they're not interested in agriculture and
rightly so.

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:32.870
It's not sustainable.

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:38.269
What we've gotta do is to put
in place a policy framework.

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:44.650
That makes agricultural,
modernizes the way that we do agriculture.

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:47.560
Looks at agriculture as an industry,

00:41:50.140 --> 00:41:56.500
sustains the incomes of a small holder
farmers, increases their income.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:02.410
And this is what we are trying to
do from the perspective of Agra.

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:09.380
We are investing and
catalyzing in small businesses.

00:42:09.380 --> 00:42:14.860
We've created over 100
seed companies in Africa.

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:17.810
And these seed companies
now produce a third

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:21.780
of all the certified seed produced
on the African continent.

00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:24.140
Phenomenal in ten years.

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:28.130
They produce enough seed to feed
15 million people every year.

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:34.150
So we're trying to help 30
million small holder, households.

00:42:35.365 --> 00:42:41.729
So the small holder farmer is
the absolute bedrock of Africa.

00:42:41.729 --> 00:42:47.155
There's nation, China, Europe,

00:42:47.155 --> 00:42:53.045
the United States, that ever got going
without sorting out their agriculture.

00:42:54.120 --> 00:42:56.270
So, to the extent that.

00:42:56.270 --> 00:43:01.930
So we've gotta put in place the right
framework from a policy perspective.

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:05.530
And that's what we work to try and
encourage governments to do.

00:43:05.530 --> 00:43:10.170
Ensure that there is a sustained domestic
investment, both from governments,

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:16.370
from entrepreneurs and
create a stable environment for farmers.

00:43:16.370 --> 00:43:23.440
Markets, ensuring to get access to seeds
and there's a whole value chain around it.

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:27.425
So there's a lot of space in agriculture.

00:43:27.425 --> 00:43:33.270
&gt;&gt; Hi Mister I'm [INAUDIBLE] from Angola.

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:37.760
Here at the GSB we talk
a lot about managerial

00:43:37.760 --> 00:43:42.220
practices that we're supposed to implement
to increase the likelihood of success.

00:43:42.220 --> 00:43:46.010
But those are mostly premised
on the existence of certain

00:43:46.010 --> 00:43:49.910
factors such as the investability
of the rule of law, or

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:52.770
the existence of a highly
qualified labor market.

00:43:54.110 --> 00:43:57.630
People that have succeeded
in the developing economies

00:43:57.630 --> 00:44:03.110
in spite of those obstacles are usually
associated with the ruling regime.

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:05.947
I think it's fair to say that
you are not one of those people.

00:44:05.947 --> 00:44:07.616
But you still succeeded.

00:44:07.616 --> 00:44:09.012
So how did you do that?

00:44:09.012 --> 00:44:13.837
And for those of us that are still
working on our first billion or

00:44:13.837 --> 00:44:17.767
million, or thousand.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:44:17.767 --> 00:44:18.547
&gt;&gt; How would you,

00:44:18.547 --> 00:44:23.435
what sort of advice would you give us?
&gt;&gt; You know

00:44:23.435 --> 00:44:27.690
somebody asked Richard Branson,
how do I become a billionaire.

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:31.660
And he said, start buying.

00:44:31.660 --> 00:44:35.116
He says how do I become a millionaire and
he says, start with a billion and

00:44:35.116 --> 00:44:38.513
buy an airline.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:44:38.513 --> 00:44:40.062
&gt;&gt; So you can keep,

00:44:40.062 --> 00:44:44.716
but you I understand where you want to go.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:44:44.716 --> 00:44:47.705
&gt;&gt; You know,

00:44:47.705 --> 00:44:52.560
it's one of my challenges when

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:56.960
I'm advising young entrepreneurs
from different African countries.

00:44:56.960 --> 00:45:00.840
Because, you know,
there are different types of governments.

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:03.280
There are different
situations on the ground.

00:45:05.950 --> 00:45:09.570
How to I advise someone from Togo and
someone in South Africa?

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:15.770
Zimbabwe and Zambia?

00:45:15.770 --> 00:45:19.250
So what I try to do is to
teach principles that will

00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:23.679
make you a success as an entrepreneur
irrespective of the conditions.

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:29.430
And one of my mantras is to say the
entrepreneur must train like a soldier.

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:34.800
We fight in the conditions,
not the conditions.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:38.480
Because we really can't wake up in
the morning and say, today it's raining,

00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:39.380
I'm not fighting.

00:45:41.250 --> 00:45:43.480
So the challenges are there.

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:49.160
But the thing is,
Africa is full of entrepreneurs.

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:54.250
Entrepreneurship is alive and
well in Africa.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:46:00.010
It is just building the support
systems to ensure that

00:46:01.480 --> 00:46:04.200
these ventures can grow in scale.

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:11.242
So I hope that as some of you get
skills from here you can take them home

00:46:11.242 --> 00:46:19.899
to be able to help others to scale.
&gt;&gt; My name is

00:46:19.899 --> 00:46:24.500
I am from Rwanda and
I remember the dismay in South Africa.

00:46:24.500 --> 00:46:26.360
I was there by the way.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:30.520
You talked about how data is actually

00:46:30.520 --> 00:46:35.560
to our generation is what internet
was to the 90's, 80's generation.

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:40.290
And with that I took at ad face but
I tried to look into that.

00:46:40.290 --> 00:46:44.470
But the problem is,
part of it data in Africa is in the air.

00:46:44.470 --> 00:46:47.150
It's not somewhere you
can actually find data.

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:49.520
And even when there is data it's so

00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:52.570
hard to break through the government
censorship actually to get that data.

00:46:54.130 --> 00:46:56.920
What do you think of,
how can you break through that?

00:46:56.920 --> 00:47:00.350
And they also have a side
question about your program.

00:47:00.350 --> 00:47:02.600
I'm part of another program
we run called Buscharunda.

00:47:02.600 --> 00:47:04.760
We come to the states, study, and go back.

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:07.740
But the issue with the program
right now is, it's so

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:09.940
hard to convince us to go back.

00:47:09.940 --> 00:47:13.110
I graduated from Stanford having
an offer from Microsoft and

00:47:13.110 --> 00:47:14.950
an offer to go back home.

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:17.160
Those two offers,
you can't even weigh them.

00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:20.410
How do you convince your students to
actually reject the offer because it's not

00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:25.480
a problem of being it's
an issue of weighing

00:47:25.480 --> 00:47:27.620
the two offers and
seeing which one is better.

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:31.990
How do you make it more attractive
to students to come back in Africa?

00:47:31.990 --> 00:47:34.101
&gt;&gt; I have a better offer you.

00:47:34.101 --> 00:47:37.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:47:37.810 --> 00:47:39.280
&gt;&gt; Go and start your own business.

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:44.957
It's better offer than Microsoft or
Intel, or Uber, or anybody, okay.

00:47:48.650 --> 00:47:53.930
Once you decide to go down that route,
none of those are good offers.

00:47:57.286 --> 00:47:59.730
So that's my challenge for you.

00:47:59.730 --> 00:48:04.380
I have a program,
which is fascinating, which

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:10.710
we take young American
graduates every year.

00:48:10.710 --> 00:48:12.490
This year we had 30.

00:48:12.490 --> 00:48:17.320
We offered not to Africans,
you can't have this one,

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:21.067
young Americans and
we send them to Africa, for

00:48:21.067 --> 00:48:27.290
three to six months, and
they work for African companies.

00:48:27.290 --> 00:48:32.207
They don't work for governments,
it's not Peace Corps, you got to go,

00:48:32.207 --> 00:48:36.270
and you got to work for
companies like mine, and guess what?

00:48:37.520 --> 00:48:40.300
They don't want to come home either, so

00:48:40.300 --> 00:48:42.150
there's something going on there,
my friend.

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:44.024
You gotta come.

00:48:44.024 --> 00:48:51.433
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:48:53.511 --> 00:48:55.000
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon.

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:56.580
My name is Daphne Sehore.

00:48:56.580 --> 00:49:00.417
I'm from Zimbabwe, and fortunately,
I'm one of your Andrew Young scholars.

00:49:00.417 --> 00:49:04.290
I graduated from
Spelman College this past May.

00:49:04.290 --> 00:49:08.690
So, I have a background in biochemistry,
and

00:49:08.690 --> 00:49:13.590
I am interested in addressing health
problems in developing countries mostly,

00:49:13.590 --> 00:49:17.830
particularly in diagnostics,
because I feel like those diseases,

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:22.788
such as Malaria, HIV,
Tuberculosis have been neglected mostly.

00:49:22.788 --> 00:49:27.670
And I'm interested in some day owning my
own biotechnology company in Zimbabwe,

00:49:27.670 --> 00:49:32.240
to address matters in Zimbabwe,
because it's very, very important for

00:49:32.240 --> 00:49:37.850
us to have such facilities
that allow us to try and

00:49:37.850 --> 00:49:41.010
solve our own problems
using our own resources.

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:46.380
So what advice would you give
a young person like myself, with

00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:52.150
no background in business, but interested
in making such changes to our continent,

00:49:52.150 --> 00:49:56.505
and to the place that we come from.
&gt;&gt; Wow, that's great.

00:49:56.505 --> 00:50:00.410
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:50:00.410 --> 00:50:04.401
&gt;&gt; We have a program with

00:50:04.401 --> 00:50:08.480
Morehouse College.

00:50:08.480 --> 00:50:10.390
So, Andrew Young came to me and

00:50:10.390 --> 00:50:14.390
said, why don't you have
a program just for Morehouse?

00:50:14.390 --> 00:50:19.330
I said okay, so we did a deal
where we would send ten students

00:50:19.330 --> 00:50:24.310
to Morehouse, but
I forgot that was only men.

00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:31.292
So we ended up doing
the identical program at Spelman,

00:50:31.292 --> 00:50:36.055
and that's where the Andrew Young Program
she talks about comes from.

00:50:36.055 --> 00:50:37.530
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:40.380
&gt;&gt; So, we send these amazing young

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:43.130
people there, and
this is the future of Africa.

00:50:45.260 --> 00:50:45.921
The.

00:50:47.767 --> 00:50:53.056
The most important thing that, look,
the challenges for you are there,

00:50:53.056 --> 00:50:58.360
but they are no different from any
generation anywhere else in the world.

00:51:00.060 --> 00:51:06.380
Okay, to the extent that you can come
home, come home and we will help you,

00:51:06.380 --> 00:51:10.470
and we will get you started in
the business that you want to get into.

00:51:11.600 --> 00:51:16.520
And really, we want to say that about,
I mean, your country,

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:21.100
Zimbabwe, which is also my country, has
just gone through an extraordinary change

00:51:21.100 --> 00:51:27.470
that the whole world witnessed, and
so there's a lot of sense of hope.

00:51:29.370 --> 00:51:32.575
So I'll see you there for
Christmas next year, how about that?

00:51:32.575 --> 00:51:38.010
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:51:38.010 --> 00:51:39.480
&gt;&gt; So I know there's still tons of

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:41.400
questions in the audience, but

00:51:41.400 --> 00:51:46.180
you'll have to post it to his Facebook
page, because we're out of time now.

00:51:46.180 --> 00:51:50.816
But I think we all agree that it's been
phenomenal, and let's give another round

00:51:50.816 --> 00:51:57.215
of applause to Strive.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:51:57.215 --> 00:52:02.160
[MUSIC]

